Bugfix: Revoked access ends the session of a sync client

Sync clients send their session cookie along with every request. After an app
password was revoked in the personal settings, a client that used it kept
syncing: requests with the revoked app password and the cookie, and requests
with the cookie alone, were answered with 207. Sessions started by a client
itself (WebDAV, HTTP Basic, OAuth2) were never checked against the credentials
they were started with, and every request extended them. Removing such a
session under "Sessions" in the settings had no effect either, its session
token was simply created again with the next request. A session started with
an OAuth2 token went on accepting requests with the cookie alone after the
desktop authorization was revoked.

These sessions are now checked before every request, like browser sessions:

- App password: the session ends with the next request after the app password
  was revoked, also when the client only sends the cookie.
- Session removed in the settings: the session ends with the next request. A
  client with valid credentials signs in again and gets a new session.
- OAuth2 and OpenID Connect: every request has to carry the Authorization
  header that the authentication module confirmed. A different header, or none
  at all, is checked by the module at once, the same header again after
  core/last_check_timeout minutes (default 5). As before, the module itself
  rejects WebDAV requests with a revoked token right away.

A session that is no longer valid is logged out and its session token is
deleted; the request continues without a login and gets 401 unless it carries
valid credentials. Requests that send only the cookie of a session started with
an OAuth2 token are no longer accepted; the desktop and mobile clients send
their token with every request. Sessions started before the update are taken
over with their first request; if that request sends only the cookie and the
session was not started with an app password, the client signs in once more.

The check reads the session token from the database as before, for app
passwords also the app password token, which the HTTP Basic login of the same
request loads anyway. The session and the token are written only when
something changes, the last activity of the token at most once a minute.
Browser sessions, public links, guests and the early release of the session
lock for sync clients are unchanged.
